      faqTitle: "Frequently asked questions about multi-language",
      q1: "Do you translate my chat messages for the driver?",
      a1: "No. There is no machine translation in Logistiq. What is translated is the interface — the form, the status labels, the buttons, the chat header, the role names. Your message text reaches him exactly as you typed it. In practice this is why short, plain messages work best: the screen around them is already in his language, so the message itself carries less weight.",
      q2: "Which twelve languages exactly?",
      a2: "Romanian, English, German, French, Polish, Italian, Spanish, Dutch, Hungarian, Bulgarian, Czech and Slovak. The list is fixed and ordered by what international transport actually brings to a Romanian yard. Each language holds every string, so nothing drops back to Romanian halfway down a screen. The same twelve are in the QRGo Driver app.",
      q3: "Does it detect the driver's language automatically?",
      a3: "No, and that is a deliberate choice. The first screen at qrgo.ro is a full-screen picker: twelve flags with native names — Română, Deutsch, Polski, Български. He taps one, and it is remembered. Guessing from a phone prefix or an IP address gets it wrong often enough — a Romanian plate with a Moldovan driver — that one tap beats one wrong guess.",
      q4: "Is the dashboard also in twelve languages?",
      a4: "Yes. The dashboard, for managers and dispatchers, comes in the same twelve languages. A visitor who is not logged in gets the language saved in the browser, otherwise the language of the country they connect from, or English; for a logged-in user the choice is saved on the account and follows them everywhere. Each side keeps its own language: the same check-in reads in Polish on the driver's phone and in Romanian on the dispatcher's screen.",
