    f17Page: {
      title: "Roles and departments control who does what",
      subtitle: "A warehouse manager creates dispatchers in their own warehouse and assigns each one to departments, with the rights they hold in each. Those departments are the dispatcher's boundary: what they see, what they may do with check-ins and bookings, and which gates reach their quick-access.",
      stepLabel: "STEP",
      uiRoles: "Roles",
      uiDepartments: "Departments",
      uiOffice: "Office · department flag",
      uiDispatcher: "Dispatcher",
      uiScope: "One warehouse",
      uiInvite: "Create dispatcher",
      uiRestricted: "Insufficient permissions",
      uiBookings: "Bookings",
      uiGates: "Gate quick-access",
      ui2fa: "2FA · TOTP or SMS",
      mxTitle: "What each role can do",
      mxSub: "Taken from the rules the product actually enforces. Warehouse manager and professional carry the same rights; a dispatcher works only inside their departments, with the rights granted in each, and without a department sees nothing.",
      mxRole1: "Warehouse manager",
      mxRole2: "Professional",
      mxRole3: "Dispatcher + departments",
      mxRole4: "Dispatcher, no departments",
      mxCap1: "Check-ins",
      mxCap2: "Departments & ramps",
      mxCap3: "Create dispatchers",
      mxCap4: "Operator check-in",
      mxCell: "YYYYYYYYPNNPNNNP",
      flowTitle: "Set up a dispatcher",
      flowSub: "The real admin path — from creating the account to the capabilities their departments open up.",
      s1t: "Create the dispatcher",
      s1d: "A warehouse manager creates the account from Users. Dispatchers are the only role they can create, and the new account inherits their warehouse automatically.",
      s2t: "Tick their departments and rights",
      s2d: "In the dispatcher's form, tick the departments they work in — Reception, Warehouse, Shipping — and, in each, what they may do with check-ins and bookings: Read, Manage, New, Delete. Saving replaces the previous set rather than adding to it.",
      s3t: "Enable operator check-in",
      s3d: "In Settings, allow operators to create check-ins manually from the dashboard and choose which departments may use it.",
      s4t: "Link gates to departments",
      s4d: "Gate quick-access follows the same link: a dispatcher gets only the active gates attached to their departments, a manager gets all of them. Opening a gate from there is in progress, along with QRGoBox.",
      s5t: "Move them, and it resets",
      s5d: "Changing a dispatcher's warehouse clears every department assignment — a deliberate stop against access that outlives the move.",
      spTitle: "Two flags that shape a department",
      spBody: "Departments are the unit of routing inside a warehouse, and two checkboxes decide how work reaches them. Entry Point marks where the check-in process begins: every driver who checks in on your qrgo.ro page lands there, and a warehouse can have at most one — set a new entry point and the previous one is cleared, with a unique index in the database making the rule impossible to break. Office / Reception marks the department carrier bookings are routed to: carriers making a booking are directed to office departments automatically. Both show as a badge in the departments table, next to the department's ramps. Neither is a role, a team or an access level — they describe the department, not the people in it.",
      spSide: "right",
      coKind: "note",
      coTitle: "Fixed roles, dispatchers bound to their departments",
      coBody: "This is a role model with per-department rights, not a role builder. You cannot compose a custom role — each role's rights are fixed in the product, checked on the server, with tenant isolation enforced by Postgres row-level security. A dispatcher is bound by their departments: in each one you grant, separately for check-ins and bookings, Read, Manage, New and Delete, and the database enforces the same grants. A dispatcher with no department sees nothing. One who may manage a check-in can still hand it over to any department of the warehouse.",
      faqTitle: "Questions about staff access",
      q1: "Can a dispatcher be limited to only their own department's check-ins?",
      a1: "Yes — that is the rule, not an option. A dispatcher sees and works on the check-ins and bookings of their departments only, enforced by Postgres row-level security, and with no department sees nothing. At most, such a dispatcher can register a waiting check-in at the entry point, and only when three things hold: you allow them to create waiting check-ins, the warehouse's Operator Check-in is on, and, if Allowed Departments lists departments, the entry-point department is among them. In each department you choose what they may do: Read, Manage, New, Delete. A dispatcher who manages a check-in can hand it over to any department of the warehouse; it then leaves their view unless they hold Read in that department.",
      q2: "Can I build a custom role or pick individual permissions for my team?",
      a2: "Not a custom role: Logistiq ships a fixed role model — warehouse manager, professional and dispatcher on the warehouse side — and each role's rights are set in the product. For a dispatcher you do pick individual rights: per department, separately for check-ins and bookings, Read, Manage, New and Delete (Manage needs Read, Delete needs Manage), and whether they may create waiting check-ins at the entry point. You also choose, in Allowed Departments, which departments may use operator check-in.",
      q3: "Who can create accounts, and what can they create?",
      a3: "A warehouse manager or professional can create dispatchers, and only inside their own warehouse — the new account inherits that warehouse automatically. A logistics admin is the role that can create warehouse managers as well as dispatchers. The rule is checked on the server, and the role-to-warehouse binding is a constraint in the database rather than a UI convention.",
      q4: "What does the Office department do — is it a role?",
      a4: "It is a flag on a department, not a role or an access level. Marking a department as Office / Reception means carriers making a booking are routed to it automatically. There is no office user and no office permission. The other flag is Entry Point: the department where the driver check-in process begins, and a warehouse can have at most one.",
      uiEntryPoint: "Entry Point",
      uiOnePer: "one per warehouse",
    },
